[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092384,
      "id": "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302",
      "title": "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=(x-2)(x^{2}+x)\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(2,0\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=(x-2)(x^{2}+x)$. Expand and combine like terms before differentiating.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=x^{3}+x^{2}-2x^{2}-2x\\\\\n&=x^{3}-x^{2}-2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3x^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(2,0\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(2)&=3\\left(2\\right)^{2}-2\\left(2\\right)-2\\\\\n&=12-4-2\\\\\n&=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(6\\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "ab4ddfa7-52df-5728-b0e1-719076325db9"
          ],
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "ab4ddfa7-52df-5728-b0e1-719076325db9",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "-\\frac{1}{6}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "f8064394-a467-5621-b670-65d8cde1af82"
        }
