    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=(x-1)(x^{2}+x)\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(-2,-6\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=(x-1)(x^{2}+x)$. Expand and combine like terms before differentiating.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=x^{3}+x^{2}-x^{2}-x\\\\\n&=x^{3}-x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3x^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(-2,-6\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(-2)&=3\\left(-2\\right)^{2}-1\\\\\n&=12-1\\\\\n&=11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(11\\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
