    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=(x-1)(x^{2}+2x)\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(-1,2\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=(x-1)(x^{2}+2x)$. Expand and combine like terms before differentiating.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=x^{3}+2x^{2}-x^{2}-2x\\\\\n&=x^{3}+x^{2}-2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3x^{2}+2x-2\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(-1,2\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(-1)&=3\\left(-1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-1\\right)-2\\\\\n&=3-2-2\\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-1\\right)}\\\\\n&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
