    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=(x-2)(x^{2}-x)\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(2,0\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=(x-2)(x^{2}-x)$. Expand and combine like terms before differentiating.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=x^{3}-x^{2}-2x^{2}+2x\\\\\n&=x^{3}-3x^{2}+2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=3x^{2}-6x+2\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(2,0\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(2)&=3\\left(2\\right)^{2}-6\\left(2\\right)+2\\\\\n&=12-12+2\\\\\n&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
